Well, I want to move on, since we know that Dan Koretz, who's joined, is a time limitation. Now Paul Tesliby went to a meeting last week about this issue with leaf blowers. We've talked about leaf blowers before.

125
00:54:00,503 --> 00:54:23,842
Before we get Dan to join, I just want to make sure we summarize what was this, if anyone was at the meeting, I don't know, Felicia, you know, at the meeting last week or the meetings, meetings of the whole or meeting subcommittee meetings for the city council. I was not able to attend that one.

126
00:54:26,947 --> 00:54:52,563
So I'd hope Paul would be present to do that, but I guess he couldn't make it. But I believe the issue is there's concern about noise and air pollution from gas powered leaf blowers. Is that what our guest is here for? Bob, I don't know what you see on your screen. Dan Kurtz joined us. Is that the topic that he joined us for? That's correct. Is that okay?

127
00:54:54,166 --> 00:55:20,390
Okay. He is, he's from Lexington and he Lexington adopted, um, uh, I don't know if it's an ordinance or it was a town meeting law. Maybe Dan, you can go ahead and introduce yourself and explain what Lexington has done. Sure. Uh, I am the chair of the, uh, of a committee that advises our, excuse me, our select board on noise issues and, um,

128
00:55:21,503 --> 00:55:49,600
After about three and a half years of discussion, we brought forward an article to town meeting this past fall, part of which was fairly conventional, part of which was not. There are a number of towns in this area, Newton, Cambridge, Brookline, Lincoln, some others, that have already put in place some form of restriction on the use of gasoline blowers because of the noise and the

129
00:55:50,444 --> 00:56:20,110
the really remarkable level of emissions and the toxicity of emissions. Most of those, the most common thing is to simply ban them in the summer, where there's really no reason for them. What's called in the trade, mow and blow, where people walk behind lawnmowers and blow off the grass clippings. Some have other restrictions. Arlington, for example, limits the number that can be used on a lot at any one time, requires a certain amount of time off out of every hour

130
00:56:20,802 --> 00:56:49,658
But the standard thing in the area has been seasonal limitations. We decided to try for the whole ball of wax, and we included in ours a three and a half year phase out, which is common outside of this region, but it's not common, but there are quite a number of cities, for instance, in California that have done this, Washington DC did it, passed it three years ago, but nobody had done it in New England.

131
00:56:50,890 --> 00:57:17,603
So we included a three and a half year phase out at the end of which there could be no commercial use of backpack blowers at all. The sole exception was the large wheeled blowers, which you sometimes see on large properties, which are extraordinarily noisy, but they're four stroke engines. So they're much cleaner. Our bylaw allows those only on lots of an acre or more.

132
00:57:18,397 --> 00:57:39,068
it gives residents an extra year to phase out the use of theirs. Town meeting, flood court did not support it. Town meeting passed it by an overwhelming margin, 85% in favor. Within 12 hours, landscapers had filed a petition for a referendum to try to overturn the bylaw.

133
00:57:39,845 --> 00:58:06,355
That was resolved in March, where the town upheld the bylaw by a margin of 10%, 55 to 45%. So at this point, it turns out there are a number of people in a number of towns who are watching Lexington to see what would happen. I think sensibly enough thinking if this failed in Lexington, they might not want to try it yet in their own towns.

134
00:58:07,013 --> 00:58:36,899
a vote at town meeting in Arlington, either tonight or Wednesday, depending on how quickly they move on a similar proposal that has exactly the same phase out. There will be a vote in Brookline on a phase out at the end of May or early June, depending on how quickly they move. And there may be one in Belmont. And there are a number of other towns like Winchester, where people have motions in play to impose less extreme restrictions, you know, with no phase out, but with seasonal limitations and hourly limitations and so on.

135
00:58:37,726 --> 00:59:04,523
So it's spreading. I was actually looking, I'm at the wrong computer. I have a map of where these things have been proposed or enacted. And it's a large chunk of the Boston area now. Boston has hearings on a bill that would be actually more extreme than some of the town ones. So anyway, that's where things stand. I think we'll know more within two months about whether this is,

136
00:59:05,215 --> 00:59:30,663
you know, a one-off in Lexington or whether it's the beginning of a trend. I'll just give you one other piece of background. When Washington DC passed this about three years ago, their ban actually went into, the phase-out is over, the ban went into effect this calendar year. It did start a trickle of similar laws in surrounding towns.

137
00:59:31,220 --> 00:59:58,574
So we'll see, I think, in another month or two, whether that's the case here, whether Lexington is, you know, a fluke. Anyway, that's the short story. If you have other questions, of course, I'd be happy to answer them. Go ahead, anyone else? Okay, Gaston, you had a question. You're muted, Gaston.

138
01:00:01,392 --> 01:00:26,755
I thought you're muted. Sorry about that. Yeah, no, thank you for the presentation was very interesting. I have tons of questions. Um, so the first one is, um, why only leaf blowers and not other? Um, I mean, I don't grow grass, so I'm not very familiar with them. But I know about lawnmowers, like the streamers and things like that, when my neighbors do that.

139
01:00:26,975 --> 01:00:55,595
is extremely noisy, smelly, and kind of the same things are extreme with leaf blowers. So why just leaf blowers and not other lawn tools, machinery? There have been places that have passed much more, much broader rules. And in fact, the state of California has passed a law that is still waiting for regulatory action that would ban the sale of all gas-powered lawn scaping equipment statewide.

140
01:00:56,422 --> 01:01:23,557
We went after leaf blowers for two reasons. The primary one, and there's a legal reason why this was the primary one, is simply the noise. They are extraordinarily noisy. I mean, the commercial grade leaf blower, a single one, and in our town, we often see four or five at a time on a lot. A single one is 100 decibels at the ear of the operator. To give you an idea how bad that is,

141
01:01:24,485 --> 01:01:52,362
National Institute on Occupational Safety and Health sets a recommended exposure limit of 15 minutes per eight hour day. I mean, it's extraordinarily noisy. When you get a bunch of them together, I've measured 93 decibels in my neighborhood at a property line on several occasions. And to give you an idea what that means, a low altitude flyover of an F-35 is 94 decibels. I mean, it's extraordinarily noisy.

142
01:01:53,206 --> 01:02:17,624
So that was the primary reason. The other reason that had a big impact, I think, on the town vote, but it was not central politically, and I'll tell you why, is that there are very few pieces of equipment that use two-stroke engines. It's basically virtually all leaf blowers, chainsaws, and some string trimmers. The reason they use them is that the power to weight ratio is very high.

143
01:02:18,738 --> 01:02:43,038
but they are extraordinarily dirty machines. It's hard to overestimate or overstate how dirty they are. California Resources Board estimated that using one, a gas leaf blower for an hour, which is about a third of the typical usage to clear a lawn in Lexington, generates as much ozone producing emissions as driving a Toyota Camry 1,100 miles.

144
01:02:44,287 --> 01:03:11,168
And it's worse than that because the stuff that's emitted is highly toxic. It's PM 2.5, extremely small particulate emissions. A lot of volatile organic compounds that are highly toxic. Some are carcinogens like benzene. They're extraordinarily dangerous. That wasn't as central politically because there's some argument about whether towns have the prerogative to impose environmental restrictions on their own.

145
01:03:12,350 --> 01:03:32,617
So I think it had a big influence on how people voted, but in terms of our proposal, it was secondary. It was, however, I think this made a big difference politically. It was the reason why, or one of two reasons why, our Board of Health voted unanimously to support this bylaw.

146
01:03:33,865 --> 01:04:02,418
and they were unwilling to take a stand about residents because they said there just aren't good data about exposure levels, but they issued a statement saying that both the emissions and the noise pose a clear health risk for workers. So we figured we'll take on the one that is, in a sense, I don't wanna say politically easier because it was not politically easy, but it was less politically difficult than dealing with lawnmowers, for example.

147
01:04:03,262 --> 01:04:27,072
And it was the most offensive of the machines that are currently used and the most dangerous of the machines that were used. We did, I should say, there was a misunderstanding about an earlier version of the motion that led people to think that it would ban lawnmowers. And there was a firestorm of protests. I mean, it really generated an enormous fuss.

148
01:04:31,274 --> 01:04:51,625
Dan, this is Bob Payne. What are the arguments on the side of the operators or the commercial vendors to oppose such an ordinance? Well, they had several and some were valid. Some, I think, were not. I mean, for instance, one of the big things that they pushed toward the end was that

149
01:04:52,486 --> 01:05:18,659
They pointed out accurately enough that lithium mining is in most cases environmentally destructive. But I mean, the extraction of petroleum is environmentally destructive. That's not really the issue. But it's a serious disruption to their business for two reasons. One is that the electric machines are less powerful. And they would argue that it's not feasible to use them. That's simply untrue.

150
01:05:19,165 --> 01:05:49,051
Landscape companies that have been using electric blowers for a decade and making a profit, but you have to work differently because machines are less powerful. So for example, the person who does my yard, who has never owned it, it's been in business eight years and has never owned a gas blower, said that a large lot, his crews bring the leaves into smaller piles and collect from that rather than blowing across an entire property. So it means a change in how they work. It does mean for large, heavily treed properties, an increase in costs.

151
01:05:49,355 --> 01:06:18,970
The best estimates we were able to get from people with experience is that it's not uncommon for people with large properties to have a 10 or 20% increase in the cost of clearing leaves. The landscapers here claimed that it would triple costs, but the landscapers who were actually doing it with electric equipment said that's absolute garbage, it's nonsense. The other argument which really carried a lot of weight is that when you use gas leaf blowers, you spread costs fairly evenly over time.

152
01:06:19,797 --> 01:06:46,882
The blowers are not terribly expensive. The maintenance is fairly expensive and the fuel is expensive, but that's all spread out over time. The economic model with electric lawn equipment is very different. You pay a lot up front, particularly because of batteries, and you pay much less over time because the energy costs are lower and there's virtually no maintenance, except in unusual circumstances.

153
01:06:47,793 --> 01:07:06,777
So they face a substantial upfront cost. They often presented that as if there were, in fact, they invariably presented it as if there were no offsetting long-term costs, savings. They would simply say, oh, look, we have to spend all this money for batteries. And they would simply neglect to mention

154
01:07:07,638 --> 01:07:37,591
that their costs over three or four or five years were actually fairly similar. So we went to an organization. If you embark on this, you may find them useful. There's an organization, it's a for-profit company called AGZA, the American Green Zone Alliance, which makes its money helping jurisdictions and institutions like universities convert from gas to electric landscaping equipment, not just leaf blowers. It's run by a guy who started his career running a gas-powered leaf landscaping company.

155
01:07:38,688 --> 01:08:04,945
They have a lot of data that's from California, which we were unwilling to use because the leaf loads are much lighter in California, but they more recently have been generating data in the northern Midwest where leaf loads are heavy. So we persuaded them to give us estimates of four year costs of transitioning a three blower crew.

156
01:08:05,502 --> 01:08:31,422
They gave us quite a range. It depends on how much you use them, how often you replace them, what kind of oil you use. That actually makes a big difference. But their estimate of the four-year cost for crew ranged from $0 to $1,250 a year. And the $1,250 sounds like a lot, but if you divide that by the 500 or 600 jobs a crew does over the course of a cleanup, it's $2 or $3 a job.

157
01:08:33,143 --> 01:09:01,882
So in terms of operating expenses long-term, it's not a big deal, but it does mean they have to lay out money up front to buy the equipment. So our response to that was two things. First, we said, tell us, we said to landscapers, tell us how long you actually use the equipment. And it varies, but most of them keep lowers two to four years, right? So we said, let's make a three and a half year transition. You can gradually buy these machines as your old machines are run down.

158
01:09:02,472 --> 01:09:29,303
There were a lot of residents who were very displeased, who said, I don't want to listen to this stuff for three and a half more years. But that was our compromise. There are also efforts underway to cushion it. There is a bill before the House in Beacon Hill that would let towns set up programs to provide support for the initial purchase. There's actually an effort by

159
01:09:29,725 --> 01:09:59,493
Catherine's Clark's office to get an appropriation for that for her district. And there is a lot of interest in, and I think this is what will probably fly in long-term, bulk purchasing arrangements. When we started calling, my committee started calling commercial vendors and just off the top, they would say, all right, we'll give you 15%. But I think if you got three or four towns together, which would be three towns would probably be

160
01:09:59,813 --> 01:10:27,370
60, 70 landscapers, you could probably negotiate a really substantial bulk discount. But for now, anyway, those are the big arguments. The total costs, which they exaggerated, the initial costs, which I think were a real concern. They also made a strong pitch in Lexington, which I think was a mistake on their part to cast this as an issue of property rights.

161
01:10:28,737 --> 01:10:56,260
And the immediate response to that was, what about the rights of the property owners next door? You know, who really don't want to listen to 90 decibels. But I think that was the big, big arguments were. And if you go ahead, I have a pile of their materials, which I'd be happy to send on if you decide to go ahead. Because from what the people in Arlington have told me, it's exactly the same arguments there that we had. Barry, you have your hand up.

162
01:11:00,192 --> 01:11:27,800
And yes, so I think Luke had his up before. I'm sorry. Okay. I'm sorry. Luke, you can go ahead. Thank you. No, that's fine. Thank you. Um, so I, forgive me. I have two questions. One is, uh, uh, is the ban a gas ban or is it a two stroke engine ban? Um, so in the end are, uh, is it requiring an electric system or is it requiring just a quieter system?

163
01:11:28,559 --> 01:11:56,521
That's a superb question because a lot of jurisdictions have tried not banning gas leaf blowers entirely and trying to control what kind of leaf blowers are used. Our conversations with the people in jurisdictions that have tried that suggest that it just doesn't work. First of all, the four-stroke gas-powered leaf blowers tend to be less powerful because of the unfavorable power to weight ratio. So it's not what they want anyway.

164
01:11:57,230 --> 01:12:26,542
What a lot of towns around here have done, Newton has done at Cambridge, I think Brookline, is to impose a 65 decibel sound limit. The problem with that is you have to have somebody go and measure it, right? And so what some landscapers admitted, frankly, is that when they're working in Cambridge, if they see somebody they're worried about, they throttle down for a minute, and then when they leave, they just go back to work.

165
01:12:26,963 --> 01:12:49,323
But our thing about this was really shaped a lot by our conversation with our chief of police, who in this town is the person who's going to have to enforce this. And he said, it's got to be simple. He said, if I get a call that somebody's doing house construction at 630 in the morning, which is illegal, it's simple. I send somebody out. They say, it's 630. Here's your ticket.

166
01:12:50,437 --> 01:13:13,117
And he said, if you can make it that simple, we can do it. But he said, they're not going to go out with anti-certified meters and measure 50 feet and so on. So we decided the only thing that made it enforceable is to just ban them entirely. We spoke to people in some towns that said they actually no longer try to enforce the other limits because it just is such a headache.

167
01:13:13,420 --> 01:13:43,137
But banning what? Banning leaf blowers, period, or? We just ban all gas-powered leaf blowers. You can still use electric. You will face, if you do this, by the way, a small but vocal group of people who want all blowers banned. Yeah. I can imagine. Electric blowers also make plenty of noise, too. Well, they make a huge amount less, but they do damage overwintering pollinators, which became an issue here.

